%S A398021 0,0,0,0,1,0,2,1,1,1,3,0,1,2,1,2,1,1,3,0,2,1,4,1,2,1,2,2,3,1,4,2,3,3,
%T A398021 5,0,2,5,3,1,3,3,1,5,3,3,6,1,7,5,2,3,5,2,6,3,5,6,4,0,6,5,4,4,3,4,4,3,
%U A398021 5,3,7,2,4,4,6,4,3,4,6,2,3,4,5,2,3,4
%N A398021 Number of values of k for which n eventually enters a non-elementary loop under a trajectory x -> x / k if k divides x and x -> x + ceiling(x / k) otherwise.
%C A398021 For a given k, define the trajectory starting at x(0) > 0 as follows:
%C A398021   x(i + 1) = x(i) / k if k divides x(i);
%C A398021   x(i + 1) = x(i) + ceiling(x(i) / k) otherwise.
%C A398021 For k = 2, this is equivalent to the Collatz step x -> x / 2 or (3 * x + 1) / 2.
%C A398021 We call a loop an 'elementary loop' if it contains 1 as a term and otherwise a 'non-elementary loop'. The loop containing 1 consists of the terms 1, 4, 2, 1 for k = 2, or 1, 2, ..., k, 1 for other k.
%C A398021 To compute a(n), it is sufficient to consider k < n. For k >= n, n is always part of the 'elementary loop'. If k >= n, then n + ceiling(n / k) = n + 1. In such case, the generated sequence is the 'elementary loop' n, n + 1, n + 2, ... k - 1, k, 1, 2, 3, ..., k - 1, k.
%C A398021 It is not known if there exists a couple (n, k) that generates an iteration growing without bound.
%H A398021 Giuseppe Ciacco, <a href="/A398021/b398021.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..9999</a>
%H A398021 Walter Carnielli, <a href="https://www.math.nthu.edu.tw/~amen/2015/AMEN(150711).pdf">Some natural generalizations of the Collatz Problem</a>, Applied Mathematics E-Notes 15 (2015): 207-215.
%F A398021 a(n) = #{k: 2 <= k <= n and the T_k-trajectory starting at n eventually enters a non-elementary loop}, where T_k(x) = x/k if k divides x, and T_k(x) = x + ceiling(x/k) otherwise.
%e A398021 For n = 7, the values k = 3 and k = 6 give non-elementary loops, while the other k with 2 <= k <= 7 do not. For k = 3 the trajectory is 7, 10, 14, 19, 26, 35, 47, 63, 21, 7, ... . Hence a(7) = 2.
%o A398021 (Python)
%o A398021 def nonloop(n,k):
%o A398021     x=n
%o A398021     seen=set()
%o A398021     while x!=1 and x not in seen:
%o A398021         seen.add(x)
%o A398021         d,r=divmod(x,k)
%o A398021         x=d if r==0 else d+x+1
%o A398021     return x!=1
%o A398021 def a(n):
%o A398021     return sum(nonloop(n,k) for k in range(2,n+1))
%o A398021 print([a(n) for n in range(1,87)])
%Y A398021 Cf. A368136.
